<Project>

  <!--
    Warnings are errors for every .NET project in this repository.

    Why one file and not five <PropertyGroup>s: the same drift that bit the two clean
    scripts and the two build scripts bites build settings too — a property added to
    four of five csprojs silently leaves the fifth permissive, and nothing reports it.

    Why this is here at all: warning TUnit0015 ("Missing TimeoutAttribute cancellation
    token parameter") shipped in 9059512 and survived until now because nothing made
    it fatal. The C library already has a mechanical zero-warning rule (the `werror`
    preset); this is the .NET counterpart.

    Scope note — the BenchmarkDotNet question, answered by reading the project BDN
    generates for a run rather than by reasoning about it: BDN writes
    BenchmarkDotNet.Autogenerated.csproj under bin/<config>/net10.0/<name>-<job>-N/,
    which is inside this tree, but it sets <ImportDirectoryBuildProps>false</
    ImportDirectoryBuildProps> and an explicit <TreatWarningsAsErrors>False</
    TreatWarningsAsErrors> of its own. Measured on macOS/arm64: that project reports
    TreatWarningsAsErrors=False, so a benchmark run is untouched by this rule and needs
    no exemption here. Every hand-written project in the repo does inherit it (all five
    verified), which is the point.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
